  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/assets/e0adac4b5e6a66f6c6a38af96f2e55c05ec98a87857af736e40d22b31fdf259b.png)\n\nThe graph of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. The points $A$ and $B$ lie on the curve and have $x$-coordinates $x=0$ and $x=3.$ Given that ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=0$ at both $A$ and $B,$ find the intervals where ${f}^{\u2032}(x)>0.$",
  "worked_solution": "We have ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=0$ at the critical points $A$ and $B.$ Note the following:\n\n- If $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, then ${f}^{\u2032}(x)>0.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing, then ${f}^{\u2032}(x)<0.$\n\nThe information about ${f}^{\u2032}(x)$ can be summarized in the following table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & (-\\infty ,0) & 0 & (0,3) & 3 & (3,\\infty ) \\\\ f & \u2198 & \\text{min} & \u2197 & \\text{max} & \u2198 \\\\ {f}^{\u2032} & - & 0 & + & 0 & -\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{\u2032}(x)>0$ on the interval $(0,3).$",
